# Safety net for anything that lands at the project root.
# (frontend/ and backend/ each have their own .gitignore too.)

node_modules/
dist/
dist-ssr/
.vite/
build/

# Never commit real environment files — only the .example templates.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Documentation is local/private reference only and never ships to GitHub.
# The repo has public remotes; docs/ carries the deployment topology, the confidential
# margin/net_price money model, security audit findings, the admin runbook, and
# docs/ANDROID.md (release signing password in plaintext).
# The two READMEs (backend/, frontend/) are the only documentation a GitHub reader sees,
# so keep those self-contained rather than linking into docs/.
docs/

# Same treatment as docs/: local/private reference only. Nothing at runtime
# reads these (no migration runner — SQL is applied by hand via the Supabase
# dashboard, see the engineering handbook), and they narrate the schema,
# money model and security fixes in more detail than belongs on a public repo.
backend/migrations/

# QA scaffolding. These carry hardcoded test passwords and call
# auth.admin.createUser / delete() directly, bypassing every control in the
# real signup path. The handbook (Part 13 §13.8.2) says they must never be
# committed and Part 13 §13.12 requires them absent from any deployed
# environment — but nothing was actually enforcing that, so a single
# `git add -A` would have pushed a working password to four remotes.
backend/scripts/_qa_*.js
# seed-shop.js hardcodes the per-SKU net_price (our margin) for named partner
# stores. That is the same confidential money model docs/ is untracked for,
# and this repo has four remotes, two of them public GitHub repositories.
backend/scripts/seed-shop.js
backend/fixtures.json
fixtures.json

# Logs & OS/editor cruft
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
.idea/
*.sw?
